(* ============================================================
   musics.ebnf — Instaparse grammar for the musics DSL
   ============================================================
   GUIDO-inspired bracket and accidental scheme, LilyPond-inspired
   leaf/command spelling otherwise.

   Bracket system:
     [ ]      Sequence     musical sequence — also reused as-is for
                             repeat/alternative's own body and a
                             VarDef's value; the walker, not the
                             grammar, decides whether a given [ ]
                             becomes a real, addressable container or
                             is spliced/stashed, based on WHERE it sits,
                             never on how it's spelled
     { }      Parallel     simultaneous parts (GUIDO's own bracket for
                             this) — can carry an Id, exactly like
                             Sequence, so a parallel group is
                             individually addressable the same way
     ^{ }     Context      named context / envelope definition — a
                             genuine Clojure map-literal echo, since a
                             Context really is just a bag of key/value
                             settings; the leading ^ sets it apart from
                             Parallel's plain { }
     '[ ]     Data         data container
     ( )      Scope        the transient body of \transpose/\reverse,
                             and a VarDef's value; never a container of
                             its own, always spliced/stashed at pop
                             time — see "Commands" below; also a slur
                             mark glued directly onto a Note/Chord (c4(
                             d4 e4)), never ambiguous with Scope, since
                             a slur's ( is always glued with no
                             separator onto an existing note/chord,
                             never a fresh element of its own; nor with
                             StructValue's own unrelated ( ) (a
                             parenthesized Value after !name:), reached
                             only from a totally different position —
                             several uses of one bracket, disambiguated
                             entirely by WHERE each is reachable

   Structural commands (transpose/repeat/alternative/grace and its four
   synonyms/reverse/chordmode) are backslash-prefixed, LilyPond-style —
   \transpose c g ( ... ), \repeat volta 2 [ ... ] — rather than Lisp
   prefix calls. repeat/alternative keep a real, retained Sequence body
   (repeat's own :source/:alternative is a genuine container with its
   own context, an Iterator replays on each pass — see "Commands"
   below); transpose/reverse are purely transient, spliced into the
   parent at pop time, and take a Scope body instead. grace's five forms
   take two bare Elements with no wrapping bracket at all. chordmode
   keeps its own dedicated ( ChordModeNote... ) body, unaffected except
   for its own now-backslash-prefixed keyword.

   Tuplets have no dedicated command at all — a note's own Duration
   carries an optional *Ratio suffix instead (c4*1/3, a triplet eighth
   spelled as a quarter run at 1/3 speed, dotted or not: c4.*2/3),
   inherited by every later note that omits its own Duration, until an
   explicit new Duration is written again (walk-time behavior — see
   flat-tree-walker — this rule only defines the syntax).

   Accidentals are GUIDO's own symbols and nothing else: # (sharp), ##
   (double sharp), & (flat), && (double flat), n (natural).

   Leaf syntax — notes, chords, rests, drums, pitches, ornaments,
   dynamics, hairpins, ties, slurs — otherwise follows real LilyPond's
   own spelling wherever LilyPond has one. A note/chord's own trailing
   tags (articulation, ornament, dynamic, hairpin, tremolo, slur) may
   repeat freely and appear in ANY order; a tie (~), if present, always
   comes last.

   Comments: ; to end of line (real Clojure), %{ ... %} for a block
   (Clojure has no block-comment reader syntax of its own to borrow, so
   this stays LilyPond-style — it was never really about LilyPond
   specifically, just a delimiter).
   ============================================================ *)

(* Every alternative NOT listed here can write directly into whatever
   context is on top of the builder stack, which at Program's own top
   level is :ROOT -- meant to be a read-only endpoint. That's still why
   Instruction/transient Command/Reference/VarRef stay excluded. repeat
   is reachable directly because an Iterator's own :source/:alternative
   is a real, retained container with a genuine context of its own,
   exactly as safe at top level as any other real container. Leaf is
   reachable directly too, for the same reason in spirit but a
   different mechanism: flat-tree-walker/walk auto-wraps a bare
   top-level Leaf in its own ordinary, auto-id'd one-child Sequence
   before walking it (the exact push-container/walk/pop-container idiom
   Sequence's own case already uses below), so a note-glued dynamic
   (c4\f) lands on that wrapper's own context, never on :ROOT's --
   exactly as safe as writing [c4\f] yourself. One real consequence:
   several bare leaves with no [ ] (c4 d4) become TWO separate one-note
   top-level Sequences, not one combined two-note Sequence -- [ ] is
   still required to group more than one Leaf together. *)
Program = ws? (TopElement (ws TopElement)*)? ws?
<TopElement> = Composite | Leaf | repeat | VarDef

<Element> = Part | Instruction | Command

<Part> = Composite | Leaf | Reference | VarRef
<Leaf> = Chord | Note | Rest | Drum | MultiRest

(* Bar / section markers -- pure structural signals for a future conductor,
   no effect on musical timing. Four levels of granularity, finest to
   coarsest: | (bar/measure) || ||| |||| (progressively larger parts).
   Reachable only through Sep/EdgeBar below (Sequence/Parallel element
   lists), not through Element/Leaf/Instruction -- so it never needs
   surrounding whitespace: c4|d4, c4 |d4, c4| d4, c4 | d4 are all one bar
   line. *)
BarLine = #'\|{1,4}'
<BarRun> = BarLine (ws? BarLine)*
<Sep> = ws
      | ws? BarRun ws?
<EdgeBar> = ws? BarRun ws?

(* ── Composites ─────────────────────────────────────────── *)

<Composite> = Context | Sequence | Parallel | Data

Context     = <'^{'> ws? (Id ws)?  Instruction (ws Instruction)* ws? <'}'>
            | <'^{'> ws? (Id ws)                                 ws? <'}'>

Sequence    = <'['>  ws? (Id ws)? EdgeBar? Element (Sep Element)* EdgeBar? ws? <']'>
            | <'['>  ws? (Id ws)                                          ws? <']'>

(* ParElement's alternatives (Context/Sequence/Reference/Instruction/
   Command) all start with a distinctive non-alphanumeric character
   (^ [ : ! \), so two of them can sit directly adjacent with no
   separator at all -- Sep is optional here, not just the bar line
   covered by EdgeBar. *)
Parallel    = <'{'>  ws? (Id ws)? EdgeBar? ParElement (Sep? ParElement)* EdgeBar? ws? <'}'>
            | <'{'>  ws? (Id ws)                                                  ws? <'}'>

Data        = <"'["> ws? (type ws)? (DataElement (ws DataElement)*)? ws? <']'>
type        = Name

<ParElement> = Context | Sequence | Reference | Instruction | Command | VarRef

(* DataElement: element for data containers *)
<ExtendedDataElement> = Atom | Primitive | Keyword | Name
<DataElement>         = Atom | Primitive
<Atom>                = Pitch | Duration | BareDuration | Articulation | BangConst | StringLit
<Primitive>           = Ratio | Float | Int

(* ── Commands ───────────────────────────────────────────── *)
(* Every one of these is a backslash-prefixed command, LilyPond-style --
   never a fresh top-level Composite of its own (repeat is the one
   exception, see TopElement above, and even it is reachable there only
   because its own body is a real retained container). An Id written
   directly inside a transient one's own body (Scope, or grace's bare
   Elements) is silently discarded, same as it always has been -- see
   CLAUDE.md's "Known rough edges". The keyword itself is hidden
   (<'\\transpose'> etc.) wherever the rule name alone already
   disambiguates it (transpose/reverse/repeat/alternative/chordmode
   each have their own distinct rule) -- but kept VISIBLE, untagged,
   for grace, where five near-identical shapes share one rule and the
   word is the only way for the walker to tell them apart. *)

(* Scope: the body of transpose/reverse below -- never registered as
   its own container, always spliced into the parent at pop time (see
   flat-core-builder/pop-container). The ws before a Scope is optional,
   not mandatory, in every command that takes one directly after its
   own keyword or last argument -- nothing that can precede a Scope
   (a Pitch, a Ratio) can itself consume Scope's own leading '(', so
   there's nothing to disambiguate by requiring a separator. *)
Scope = <'('> ws? (Id ws)? EdgeBar? Element (Sep Element)* EdgeBar? ws? <')'>

transpose   = <'\\transpose'> ws from-pitch ws to-pitch ws? Scope
from-pitch  = Pitch
to-pitch    = Pitch

(* reverse -- retrograde, order only: pitches/durations/timing on each
   child are untouched, just the sequence they come in. Same silent-
   skip limitation transpose already has (see this file's own header
   comment on transient commands and CLAUDE.md's "Known rough edges"):
   a nested container reference among the body's own children reorders
   along with everything else at this level, but its OWN internal
   content is never recursed into or itself reversed. *)
reverse     = <'\\reverse'> ws? Scope

(* chordmode -- LilyPond's own compact chord shorthand, root:quality
   (c4:m7), scoped inside its own ( ... ) body rather than reachable as
   a bare note suffix: root:quality's own ':' collides, unavoidably,
   with the existing Tremolo suffix (Tremolo = <':'> Int accepts ANY
   integer, not just powers of two -- confirmed directly, not assumed
   -- so c4:7 is genuinely ambiguous between "tremolo subdivision 7" and
   "dominant seventh chordmode" with nothing left over either way to
   disambiguate). Real LilyPond itself never allows this shorthand bare
   either -- only inside its own \chordmode {...}/\chords block -- so
   scoping it the same way here is the faithful spelling, not a
   workaround dressed up as one. Deliberately narrow for now (phase 1):
   a flat run of ChordModeNote entries only (no nested Sequence/
   Composite inside the block), and only the common-chord modifier
   vocabulary below -- see common.music-data/chord-qualities for the
   exact interval table, and that ns's own docstring for what's
   deliberately still out of scope (9/11/13, LilyPond's general
   additive .step(+/-) alteration syntax). Each entry walks straight to
   the SAME multi-pitch Leaf shape an explicit <c e g> Chord already
   produces (flat-tree-walker/walk-chord-mode-note) -- a chordmode
   chord and a spelled-out one are indistinguishable once committed. *)
chordmode = <'\\chordmode'> ws? <'('> ws? EdgeBar? ChordModeNote (Sep ChordModeNote)* EdgeBar? ws? <')'>

(* ChordQuality? -- LilyPond's own bare-note default: a plain pitch with
   NO ':' at all still means a full major triad inside chordmode
   (Notation Reference: "None: produces a major triad"), not "just a
   single note" the way the identical text would read OUTSIDE this
   block -- see walk-chord-mode-note's own default when no :Quality
   child is present.

   ChordModeNoteSuffix, NOT the general NoteSuffix -- Tremolo dropped:
   confirmed live, not just reasoned about, that ChordQuality being
   OPTIONAL reopens the exact ':Int' collision the whole \chordmode(...)
   wrapper exists to avoid in the first place, for every NUMERIC quality
   (:5/:7/:9/:11/:13) specifically -- 'C4:5' parses two genuinely valid
   ways once quality can be skipped: ChordQuality "5", or (quality
   absent, bare major triad) + a trailing Tremolo suffix ':5' -- and
   instaparse silently picked the second, wrong one every time. Ordering
   the Quality regex differently cannot fix this: it's a choice between
   two DIFFERENT rules at the same position, not a regex-alternative
   prefix issue. Dropping Tremolo from chordmode entries removes the
   ambiguity structurally instead -- also the musically sensible
   choice, since a rhythmic tremolo marking on a lead-sheet chord symbol
   isn't a meaningful thing to write in the first place. Tie, same as
   every other leaf shape now, is forced to the very end rather than
   living in this alternation -- see NoteSuffix below for the general
   reasoning. *)
<ChordModeNoteSuffix> = Modifier | Ornament | Dynamic | Hairpin | SlurMark

ChordModeNote = Pitch Duration ChordQuality? ChordBass? Articulation? ChordModeNoteSuffix* Tie?
              | Pitch          ChordQuality? ChordBass? Articulation? ChordModeNoteSuffix* Tie?

(* Quality: LilyPond's own common-chord + extended-chord modifier
   words, not invented (see common.music-data/chord-qualities for the
   exact interval each one resolves to). Longer alternatives listed
   before their own prefixes for the same reason Accidental's own regex
   already documents: alternation commits to the first match, not the
   longest -- maj13/maj11/maj9/maj7 before bare maj (which is itself a
   PREFIX of every one of those and must also precede the whole m*
   family below, 'm' being a prefix of 'maj' too), dim7 before dim,
   m13/m11/m9/m7/m6 before bare m. *)
<ChordQuality> = <':'> Quality ChordAddition* ChordRemoval?
Quality      = #'maj13|maj11|maj9|maj7|maj|dim7|dim|aug|sus2|sus4|m13|m11|m9|m7|m6|m|13|11|9|5|6|7|3'

(* LilyPond's own general alteration mechanism (Notation Reference,
   "Extended and altered chords"), applied ON TOP of whatever Quality
   already built rather than duplicating its named words -- this is
   what a fixed table of named qualities alone could never reach
   (aug7, m7.5- (half-diminished), m7+ (minor-major7), c:9^3 ("no3"),
   c:13.11 (explicitly restoring the 11 a plain :13 drops by default),
   ...), all for free once add/override/remove work by scale STEP
   NUMBER against common.music-data/chord-qualities' own step-keyed
   entries. 'Additions follow the extent and are prefixed by a dot' --
   each one OVERRIDES any existing entry for that same step number
   (LilyPond: 'later entries for a given step override earlier
   entries' -- confirmed via LilyPond's own worked example, c:3.5.5-.5+
   resolving to an augmented triad, not some cumulative-adjustment
   result), computed fresh from common.music-data/chord-step-defaults
   every time, never relative to whatever was there before. 'Following
   any steps to be added, a series of steps to be removed is
   introduced... with a prefix of ^... separated by . following the
   initial ^' -- one ^, then a dot-separated list, matching ChordBass's
   own reuse-Pitch precedent: reuse Step rather than inventing a
   second numeric rule. *)
ChordAddition = <'.'> Step ('+' | '-')?
ChordRemoval  = <'^'> Step (<'.'> Step)*
Step          = #'1[13]|[2-9]'

(* Bass note / inversion -- LilyPond's own /pitch and /+pitch (Notation
   Reference, "Chords in other parts", adding a bass note): plain
   /pitch, when that pitch class is already part of the chord, MOVES it
   to the bottom rather than duplicating it (a genuine inversion);
   /+pitch always adds a new note regardless -- both exactly LilyPond's
   own documented distinction, not a new one. Reuses the same Pitch
   rule as the root, so a relative bass note continues the same
   relative-pitch chain any other written pitch would. *)
ChordBass = <'/'> '+'? Pitch

(* repeat/alternative keep a real, retained Sequence body (unlike
   Scope-bodied transpose/reverse above) -- repeat's own :source/
   :alternative is a genuine container with its own context, an
   Iterator replays it on each pass. repeat/tremolo are one rule:
   repeat-type (unfold/volta/tremolo) picks the variant, all three
   requiring a Sequence body uniformly. *)
repeat       = <'\\repeat'> ws repeat-type ws count ws Sequence (ws alternative)?
<repeat-type> = 'volta' | 'unfold' | 'tremolo'
count        = Int
alternative  = <'\\alternative'> ws Sequence

(* Grace Notes -- five forms, each taking two bare Elements (no
   wrapping bracket at all), order differing by keyword:
     \grace/\acciaccatura/\appoggiatura/\slashedGrace  -- grace-note main-note
     \afterGrace                                       -- main-note grace-note *)
grace = '\\grace'        ws Element ws Element
      | '\\acciaccatura' ws Element ws Element
      | '\\appoggiatura' ws Element ws Element
      | '\\slashedGrace' ws Element ws Element
      | '\\afterGrace'   ws Element ws Element

<Command> = transpose | repeat | grace | reverse | chordmode

(* ── Variables ──────────────────────────────────────────── *)
(* name = [ ... ] defines a variable (Sequence's own [ ] bracket, reused
   as-is -- the walker sees a Sequence node sitting in VarDef's own
   value position and, on that basis alone, stashes it under the name
   rather than registering it as an addressable container), \name
   references it, must be defined first. See src/input/CLAUDE.md's
   "Comments and variables" section for the full reasoning. *)
VarDef = VarName ws? <'='> ws? Sequence
VarRef = <'\\'> VarName

(* Ornament names AND every reserved command word (\transpose/\repeat/
   \alternative/\grace and its four synonyms/\reverse/\chordmode) share
   VarRef's own \name spelling -- the genuine collision: a bare \trill
   or \transpose has to be structurally incapable of also parsing as
   VarRef, not just conventionally discouraged from being used that
   way. Verified directly against instaparse, not assumed elsewhere in
   this file: its own ambiguity resolution for a genuinely ambiguous
   grammar is not reliable declaration order, so this exclusion has to
   be structural (a negative lookahead), not positional. *)
VarName = #'(?!(prallmordent|prallprall|lineprall|prallup|pralldown|downprall|prall|upmordent|downmordent|mordent|trill|turn|reverseturn|verylongfermata|longfermata|shortfermata|fermata|transpose|repeat|alternative|acciaccatura|appoggiatura|slashedGrace|afterGrace|grace|reverse|chordmode)\b)[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*'

(* ── Instructions ───────────────────────────────────────── *)

(* \partial <duration> -- LilyPond's own literal spelling (\partial 8),
   not a !-prefixed Assignment like !Meter:/!tempo: -- every other
   Instruction in this grammar sets an ordinary, ramping-capable context
   value, but \partial is a pure structural declaration (affects bar-
   boundary accounting only, never a sounding duration) with no real
   LilyPond equivalent among this DSL's own !key:value instructions, so
   it keeps LilyPond's own backslash-command spelling instead of being
   folded into that family just for consistency. Walked to a plain,
   :fixed context value under :Partial (core.domain.resolve/
   common-keys+defaults samples it per leaf, same batched pass :Meter
   already rides in) -- a voice applies it ONCE, against whatever's
   active on its own very first leaf, to seed where its first :bar
   crossing actually falls (see core.async-engine's own comment on
   :partial-pending?). *)
<Instruction> = BangConst | KeyAssignment | Assignment | Invalidate | Partial

Partial = <'\\partial'> ws Duration

BangConst     = <'!'> Name
KeyAssignment = <'!key:'> KeySpec
(* Assignment excludes the literal name "key" via AssignName, same
   negative-lookahead pattern VarName uses to exclude reserved words
   above -- instaparse's ambiguity resolution between this rule and
   KeyAssignment is not reliable declaration order, so !key:<tonic> is
   genuinely ambiguous whenever the tonic is letters-only unless this
   exclusion forces KeyAssignment to win. *)
Assignment    = <'!'> AssignName Ramp
              | <'!'> AssignName <':'> Value RampMark?

AssignName = #'(?!key\b)[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*'
(* Invalidate: counterpart of ! -- !mf sets a context value, !/mf marks
   it no-longer-active from here on, so the search falls through to
   whatever the enclosing context would say instead (see
   core.domain.context/ctx-invalidate). Unambiguous vs. BangConst/
   Assignment: Name never starts with '/', so '!/mf' can only ever be
   this rule, never '!' + a Name reading "/mf". *)
Invalidate    = <'!/'> Name

(* SignedFloat/SignedInt (not the plain Float/Int used everywhere else --
   durations, octaves, repeat counts, a Ramp's own DurationExpr --
   none of which should ever go negative) are the only numeric literals
   that accept an explicit leading sign, used just here and in Target
   below. A context value genuinely can be negative (panning -1.0..1.0,
   transposition, bend, micro-timing, ...) -- an explicit '+' is accepted
   too, purely for symmetry with '-' when an author wants to write it
   explicitly (e.g. alternating panning values, !pan:-1.0/!pan:+1.0). *)
<Value> = TempoMark | Ratio | SignedFloat | SignedInt | StringLit | QualifiedName | StructValue

(* A tempo marking, LilyPond-style: note-value = BPM, e.g. !tempo:4=120
   (quarter note = 120bpm) or !tempo:3/8=120 (dotted quarter = 120bpm --
   a bare Int note-value N means 1/N, an explicit Ratio is taken as-is).
   Only meaningful for the tempo-family keys, but parses as a generic
   Value so any assignment could in principle use it. *)
TempoMark = (Int | Ratio) <'='> Int

(* ── Ramp ───────────────────────────────────────────────── *)
(*
   A Ramp is a Value in its own right, but unlike every other Value it
   attaches directly to the assignment name with no colon -- the ramp's
   own leading < or > reads as the separator instead. The curve prefix
   (if any) comes right after the direction, not before it as glued to
   the name -- Name is a greedy letter regex, so a prefix glued onto the
   name (`!vols<...`) is ambiguous about where the name ends; anchored
   after < or >, a letter there can only ever be the curve prefix.
   Four forms:
   - Open-ended, no curve:    !vol<          direction only, no target
   - Open-ended, with curve:  !vol<s         curve set now, target later
   - Timed, no curve:         !vol<16:ff     duration + target
                              !vol<16*4:ff   duration as product
                              !vol<16/1:ff   duration as ratio
                              !pan<4:1.0     float target
                              !tempo>8:60    int target
   - Timed, with curve:       !vol<s:16:ff   curve, duration, target --
                              a ramp point really is this (curve, duration,
                              target) triple; the plain timed form just
                              defaults curve to linear.

   No colon is needed between the curve letter and the duration --
   CurvePrefix is always a single letter and DurationExpr always starts
   with a digit, so the two token classes never collide -- but the timed
   form keeps one for readability, matching a (curve, duration, target)
   triple read left to right. Assignment itself never contributes a
   colon for this Value type; the only colon(s) here are internal to
   Ramp.
*)

Ramp         = RampMark
             | Direction              DurationExpr <':'> Target
             | Direction CurvePrefix <':'> DurationExpr <':'> Target

(* RampMark: direction (+ optional curve), with no value/duration/target
   of its own -- Ramp's own bare, open-ended form, and also (see
   Assignment above) glued directly onto a value in one instruction,
   e.g. !vol:mf< -- sets volume to mf AND marks this point as a ramp-
   start, in one instruction, instead of needing a separate !vol<
   right after. *)
<RampMark>   = Direction CurvePrefix?

DurationExpr = DurationAtom (<'*'> DurationAtom)*
DurationAtom = Ratio | Int

Target       = DynamicMark | SignedFloat | SignedInt

DynamicMark  = 'pppp' | 'ppp' | 'pp' | 'p' | 'mp' | 'mf'
             | 'ffff' | 'fff' | 'ff' | 'f'

(* StructValue keeps its own ( ) -- see the header comment's
   bracket-system note on why this never collides with a slur or a
   Scope, despite all three sharing the same bracket. *)
StructValue   = <'('> ws? (ExtendedDataElement (ws ExtendedDataElement)*)? ws? <')'>
QualifiedName = Name (<'.'> Name)*
KeySpec       = #'[A-Ga-g][b#]?(?:\.[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)*'

<CurvePrefix> = 'l' | 's' | 'i' | 'o'
<Direction>   = '<' | '>'

(* ── Notes ──────────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Note  = Pitch Duration NoteSuffix* Tie?
      | Pitch           NoteSuffix* Tie?

(* Split by letter case -- absolute vs. relative resolution
   (leaf-parser/resolve-pitch) already keys off case, and here it's
   load-bearing at the grammar level too: only an absolute (uppercase)
   note may take a bare-digit OctaveAbs (see the comment below), never
   a relative (lowercase) one, where a bare digit must always be a
   Duration -- LilyPond-style relative octave shifts are ' / , ticks
   only, never a digit. This has to be two genuinely different letter
   rules, not one shared PitchLetter both alternatives reference --
   sharing one wouldn't gate anything, since Pitch's own choice of
   OctaveAbs? vs. OctaveTicks? has no way to see which charset a shared
   rule happened to match internally. PitchLetterAbs/PitchLetterRel tag
   differently (not both as PitchLetter) -- flat-tree-walker's
   pitch-tuple and the inline :Pitch case check for either tag. *)
Pitch          = PitchLetterAbs Accidental? OctaveAbs?
               | PitchLetterRel Accidental? OctaveTicks?
PitchLetterAbs = #'[A-G]'
PitchLetterRel = #'[a-gp]'

(* Accidental: GUIDO's own symbols, and nothing else -- # (sharp), ##
   (double sharp), & (flat), && (double flat), n (natural). ## and &&
   are listed before their own single-character prefixes (# and &)
   since regex alternation commits to the first alternative that
   matches, not the longest -- same reasoning this file's Quality/Step/
   ChordQuality regexes already document elsewhere. Resolved to a
   semitone offset by leaf-parser/accidental-semitones. *)
Accidental  = #'##|&&|[#&n]'

(* An absolute note is always a letter-octave compound -- C4 is octave 4,
   never "C, duration 4" -- so the octave digit is mandatory, but the
   trailing '/' is only there to disambiguate it from an immediately-
   following duration digit (C4/2 = octave 4, duration 2; without the
   slash, C42 would be unparseable as "octave 4, duration 2" -- the
   digits would just run together into Duration's own 1-2-digit
   NumDuration, "octave 4 no duration, duration 42"). Written as
   C4 alone (nothing, or a non-digit, immediately after), the slash
   isn't needed at all and may be omitted. *)
<Octave>    = OctaveAbs | OctaveTicks
OctaveAbs   = #'[1-8](?:/|(?!\d))'
OctaveTicks = #"'+|,+"

(* ── Duration ───────────────────────────────────────────── *)

<Duration>      = DurationSpecial | DurationNum
DurationSpecial = #'\\longa' | #'\\breve'
(* Up to 3 digits, not 2 -- a 128th note (a fast ornamental passage)
   needs it; the walker's own digit->fraction conversion (flat-tree-
   walker's parse-duration, a plain (/ 1 n)) handles any N generically,
   so this regex's own cap is the only real limit. An optional trailing
   DurationRatio (see below) scales the whole thing -- c4.*1/3 is a
   dotted quarter at 1/3 speed, the dot still part of this same regex,
   matched before the ratio. *)
DurationNum     = #'[1-9][0-9]{0,2}\.*' DurationRatio?

(* DurationRatio -- this DSL's entire answer to the tuplet problem, no
   separate \tuplet/\times command needed: a plain *Ratio suffix scales
   the base duration directly (c4*1/3, a triplet eighth spelled as a
   quarter run at 1/3 speed), for any ratio, not just thirds. A later
   note that omits its own Duration entirely still inherits whatever
   base duration AND ratio the previous note last wrote, until an
   explicit new Duration appears -- that inheritance is walk-time
   behavior (flat-tree-walker), this rule only defines the syntax
   itself. *)
DurationRatio   = <'*'> Ratio

(* BareDuration: a duration value with no pitch attached, e.g. /4, /8,
   /16., authored as pure data inside a Data container -- a talea (an
   isorhythmic duration cycle), the durational counterpart of a bare
   Pitch atom (a color/pitch cycle, [c d e f g a b]). Reuses the exact
   same Duration spelling as a Note's own duration digit (DurationNum/
   DurationSpecial, DurationRatio included) -- no new duration syntax
   invented, just a leading '/' to mark "this atom is a duration
   alone". The '/' is load-bearing, not decorative: a bare digit inside
   a Data container already means Int (DataElement's own Primitive
   alternative), so an unmarked "4" would be ambiguous between Int and
   Duration -- '/4' can only ever be this. *)
BareDuration    = <'/'> Duration

(* ── Articulation ───────────────────────────────────────── *)

Articulation          = <'-'> ArticulationShorthand
                      | <#'\\'> ArticulationName
ArticulationName      = 'staccato' | 'staccatissimo' | 'tenuto' | 'marcato' |
                        'portato'  | 'accent'         | 'espressivo'
ArticulationShorthand = #'[-.>^_!+]'

(* ── Modifiers, Ornaments, Tremolo ──────────────────────── *)

OrnamentName = 'prall'     | 'prallup'      | 'pralldown'   | 'upprall'     |
               'downprall' | 'prallprall'   | 'lineprall'   | 'prallmordent'|
               'mordent'   | 'upmordent'    | 'downmordent' |
               'trill'     | 'turn'         | 'reverseturn' |
               'shortfermata' | 'fermata'   | 'longfermata' | 'verylongfermata'

Ornament = <#'\\'> OrnamentName
Modifier = <#'\\'> Name <':'> ModValue
Tremolo  = <':'> Int

(* A dynamic mark glued directly onto a note/chord, LilyPond-style
   (c4\f, <c e g>4\mf) -- reuses the same DynamicMark rule as Ramp's
   Target, so it's the identical word list a bare !f/!mf BangConst
   accepts. Distinguished from Modifier (which always requires a
   ':' + value) since a bare backslash-name with no colon can only
   ever be this or Ornament/ArticulationName's fixed word lists, none
   of which overlap with DynamicMark's.

   The optional trailing Direction (c4\mf<, no second '\') is the same
   "value + ramp-start in one token" idea as Assignment's own Value
   RampMark? (!vol:mf<) -- chaining a direction glued straight onto the
   mark, not as its own separate Hairpin suffix requiring a second '\'.
   Only the direction, no CurvePrefix -- apply-note-dynamics! (see the
   walker) has never supported a curve here, glued or chained. Bare (no
   '\'), unlike Hairpin's own leading one below, because it's never
   ambiguous with a separate Hairpin suffix: Hairpin always requires its
   own leading '\', so a bare '<'/'>' straight after DynamicMark can only
   ever be this. c4\mf\< (the two-backslash spelling -- Dynamic with no
   trailing direction, then a separate Hairpin) parses too, unchanged --
   this is a shorter alternative spelling of the same thing, not a
   replacement for it. *)
Dynamic  = <#'\\'> DynamicMark Direction?

(* A hairpin start glued onto a note/chord, LilyPond-style (c4\<, c4\>) --
   opens a crescendo/decrescendo from this note onward, same direction-only
   meaning as an open-ended !vol</!vol> Assignment Ramp. Always keeps its
   own leading '\' (unlike Dynamic's trailing Direction above) -- this is
   LilyPond's own spelling for a hairpin (c4\<, c4\>), not something this
   grammar invented, so it stays exactly as LilyPond writes it whether or
   not a Dynamic precedes it. Chainable directly after a Dynamic suffix
   with no separator too (c4\mf\<, see Dynamic above for the shorter
   c4\mf<) since NoteSuffix already repeats freely -- see
   apply-note-dynamics! in the walker for how the pair combine either
   way. *)
Hairpin  = <#'\\'> ('<' | '>')

<ModValue>   = Float | Int | StringLit | Name

(* Tremolo is a note/chord suffix: c4:32, <c e g>4:32
   Measured tremolo (\repeat tremolo N [...]) is a Command.

   NoteSuffix -- every trailing tag a Note/Chord can carry EXCEPT Tie,
   which is deliberately NOT a member of this alternation: these seven
   repeat freely and in ANY order (c4\prallmordent\f-., c4-.\f
   \prallmordent, ... all equally valid), but Tie is pulled out to its
   own dedicated Note/Chord suffix, Tie?, always the very last thing
   after every other tag -- see Note/Chord below. *)
<NoteSuffix> = Articulation | Modifier | Ornament | Dynamic | Hairpin | Tremolo | SlurMark

(* Slur, LilyPond-style: attaches directly to the note it starts/ends on
   (c4( d4 e4)), not a separate bracketed instruction -- ( ) means ONLY
   this and StructValue's own unrelated '(' ')' (a parenthesized data
   list, !key:(...)) anywhere in this grammar. No ambiguity between the
   two: StructValue's parens only ever open at a fresh Value position
   (always after a Sep/ws/a command's own leading tokens), while a
   SlurMark is only reachable glued directly onto a Note/Chord's own
   trailing suffixes, with no separator at all. *)
SlurMark = '(' | ')'

(* ── Tie ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── *)

(* Tie (~) is always the LAST tag on a Note/Chord, if present at all --
   Note = Pitch Duration NoteSuffix* Tie? below, never inside
   NoteSuffix's own repeated alternation, so c4-.\f~ parses but
   c4~-.\f does not: the walker's own tie check no longer needs to
   scan for a :Tie-tagged child anywhere among a Note's children, since
   grammar now guarantees it can only ever be the final one. *)
Tie = '~'

(* ── Chord ───────────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Chord = '<' !'<' ChordPitches '>' Duration NoteSuffix* Tie?
      | '<' !'<' ChordPitches '>'          NoteSuffix* Tie?

(* p (PitchLetterRel's own rhythm-only Pulse letter -- see
   input.reader.flat-tree-walker/pulse-letter?) is excluded here, not
   just at walk time: a chord's own pitches must all be real ones, so
   <c e p>4 is a genuine parse error (instaparse's own line/column),
   not something that reaches the walker at all -- ChordPitch's
   negative lookahead is checked ahead of Pitch itself, so it rejects
   the letter regardless of which Pitch alternative would otherwise
   match it. ChordPitch stays hidden (<...>) for the same reason
   ChordPitches already is -- Pitch's own node still splices straight
   into Chord's children, no extra wrapper level for the walker to see. *)
<ChordPitches> = ChordPitch ws ChordPitch (ws ChordPitch)*
<ChordPitch>   = !'p' Pitch

(* ── Rest ────────────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Rest = 'r' Duration
     | 'r'

(* ── MultiRest ───────────────────────────────────────────── *)
(* R, LilyPond's own multi-measure rest -- superset of real LilyPond in
   one specific way (see input.reader.flat-tree-walker/walk-multi-rest):
   with an explicit Duration, this is EXACTLY LilyPond's own R1*4 (the
   composer picks the note-value matching one bar in the current meter,
   same responsibility a bare r's own duration already carries, then
   *n multiplies it) -- real LilyPond doesn't derive this from the
   meter automatically either, despite the name. With NO Duration at all
   (R, or R*4), this DSL derives one bar's length from whatever Meter is
   actually active at this point and multiplies by n -- a genuine
   extension with no LilyPond equivalent, since a bare whole-note rest
   isn't actually one bar in most meters (e.g. 3/8). Either way *n
   defaults to 1 when omitted. *)
MultiRest = 'R' Duration <'*'> Int
          | 'R' Duration
          | 'R' <'*'> Int
          | 'R'

(* ── Drum ────────────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Drum    = 'x' Duration DrumMod?
        | 'x'          DrumMod?
DrumMod = <#'\\'> (Int | Name)

(* ── Primitives ──────────────────────────────────────────── *)

Name        = #'[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*'
Float       = #'[0-9]+\.[0-9]+'
Int         = #'[0-9]+'
Ratio       = #'[0-9]+/[0-9]+'
SignedFloat = #'[+-]?[0-9]+\.[0-9]+'
SignedInt   = #'[+-]?[0-9]+'
StringLit = <'"'> #'[^"]*' <'"'>
Keyword   = <':'> #'[a-zA-Z0-9_][a-zA-Z0-9_\-]*'
Id        = Name <':'>
Reference = <':'> Name

(* ── Whitespace and comments ────────────────────────────── *)
(* ; to end of line (real Clojure line comments) and %{ ... %} for a
   block. The two never compete for the same leading character, so
   there's no ambiguity to guard against between them. *)
Comment = #'%\{[\s\S]*?%\}'
        | #';[^\n]*'
<Blank> = #'\s+'
<ws>    = (Blank | Comment)+
